Approvals it can explain, a real reset, and a mic and a speaker
===============================================================

The composer's approval control is one button that names itself — `APPROVALS ⌄` — opening a menu of
three modes with a line of plain English under each. The old pair made the caption look like the
label and the value look like the control, and the caret sat on top of the word because a generic
`.composer-tools select` padding rule flattened its gutter.

**Smart is a record, not a mood.** Every approval you grant is fingerprinted (tool plus what it acts
on) and the last 200 are kept. In Smart a request matching something already allowed goes through
without a card; anything new still stops and asks. Manual asks every time. YOLO never asks, and the
engine now accepts `bypassPermissions` — the approval path already understood it and only the
session validator was refusing it, so the third mode could not be offered even though it worked.

**Mic and Speaker replace one Dictate button and one Speak button.** Each is a device list with Off
as a row, because "which input" is the question people actually have and on/off falls out of
answering it. With both live the toolbar says **Conversation**, which is the honest name for it. The
microphone drives the same dictation control that already existed — the list chooses the input, the
existing engine path does the work.

**Speak is wired to the engine, not the webview.** WebKitGTK implements none of the Web Speech API,
so `speechSynthesis` is `undefined` on Linux and a Speak button built on it is decorative. Replies
are chunked by sentence and sent to `/api/quixi/voice/speak`, which is synchronous per chunk — that
is what lets Stop take effect between sentences instead of after the whole reply.

**Resizing, honestly.** The reading column was capped at 740px inside a padding calculation, so no
window size ever widened the text: it is `--chat-width` now, with a grab strip on the column's right
edge. The task list and the composer resize on both axes. No corner ticks are drawn — the drag
regions are transparent and the cursor does the talking.

**A dev door that actually resets.** Onboarding (development) used to reopen the wizard over a live
install, which is a rehearsal and not a first run: a stranger has no keys. It asks, then calls
`POST /api/quixi/first-run/reset` → `ProviderManager::reset_first_run()`, clearing every provider's
key, both OAuth tokens, the account label and the tested flags, unsetting the active provider and
model, and marking setup unanswered. Verified in a container by seeding a live key into
`providers.json`, clicking both buttons through the automation bridge, and reading the file back:
onboarding false, active provider empty, zero configured providers, key gone.

**Smaller things that are still things.** The provider dropdown sorts alphabetically instead of by
catalog rank, because a list is scanned in order. The memory folder says what it means: "append-only:
the app never deletes. You can still delete the files yourself in any file manager."

